title: Security
kind: overview
audience: dev
aliases: auth authentication
summary: A single-user tool on loopback: what it defends against — web pages, crafted media, the network — and what it does not.

metafolder is single-user and local: the daemon and the GUI server listen on
`127.0.0.1` only, and neither talks to the internet. What it defends against:

* ''Web pages in your browser.'' Any page can send requests to `127.0.0.1:<port>`. Every request to the daemon, and every sensitive request to the GUI server, must carry a session token only your own processes can read ([[Session tokens]], [[Why a session token]]).
* ''Crafted files.'' Images, videos and PDFs are decoded just by appearing in a panel; every decoder runs sandboxed, and the GUI refuses to start without the sandbox ([[Sandboxing the media decoders]]).
* ''The network.'' Nothing in metafolder can reach a remote host, the WebView included ([[No network access]]).

What it does not defend against: another user of the machine is kept out of the
token files, but malware already running as ''you'' can read them, and can do
anything you can anyway. There is no multi-user model, and no encryption of
loopback traffic.

